  <h1>Common Salary Negotiation Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Negotiating will make me look greedy.&rdquo;</h2><p>Employers expect negotiation. Hiring managers who make offers know the number will likely be countered. A professional, data-backed counter demonstrates confidence and self-awareness — qualities employers value. Studies consistently show that negotiating has no significant effect on whether an offer is accepted or relationship quality afterward.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;They might rescind the offer if I negotiate.&rdquo;</h2><p>Rescinding an offer for professional negotiation is extremely rare — it would expose the employer to reputational risk and legal scrutiny. Offers are rescinded for legitimate conduct violations or misrepresentations, not for asking for more money professionally.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;My current salary is my ceiling.&rdquo;</h2><p>Your current salary reflects one employer&rsquo;s assessment at one point in time. Market rates change. Skills appreciate. A new employer is assessing your market value today — not what your last employer paid. Salary history is increasingly legally protected from disclosure in many states precisely because it perpetuates pay compression.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I should wait until after I start to negotiate benefits.&rdquo;</h2><p>The best time to negotiate everything — salary, signing bonus, equity, PTO — is before you accept. Once you start, your leverage disappears. Changes after starting require internal processes, manager support, and often a performance cycle. Negotiate everything up front.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The published salary range is the most they&rsquo;ll pay.&rdquo;</h2><p>Published ranges are often low-end anchors. Exceptional candidates and those with competing offers regularly get offers above the stated range (especially as signing bonuses). The posted range is a starting point for conversation, not a hard ceiling.</p>
